  "narrative_body": "A drone struck the electrical infrastructure at the Barakah nuclear plant last week, the most provocative attack of the Iran conflict — and the UAE did not strike back.\n\nThat choice defines the week. Rather than acknowledge any military response, the UAE routed everything through institutions: an emergency UN Security Council session on May 19, where IAEA Director General Rafael Grossi warned that a direct hit on the plant \"could result in a very high rate of radioactivity to the environment\"; a formal demand that Iraq \"immediately and unconditionally halt and prevent all acts of aggression\" from its soil; and a four-capital European tour by Abdullah bin Zayed, the foreign minister, who extracted public condemnations from the UK, Germany, Luxembourg, and the Czech Republic. The diplomatic framing was equally precise — attributing the drones to Iraqi territory and Iran's militia proxies rather than to the Iranian state directly, preserving room for the peace negotiations that, by May 24, the US president was describing as \"largely negotiated.\"\n\nThe decision not to retaliate publicly was itself a signal. The UAE invoked \"full sovereign, legal, diplomatic, and military rights to address any threat,\" intercepted six further drones in the 48 hours after the initial strike, and then did nothing visible. That pattern — invoke, intercept, abstain — confirms the deniability posture is not situational. It held under the hardest test of the conflict.\n\nEven as the UAE worked the institutional track, peace negotiations lurched forward. The shift from \"massive life support\" on May 17 to \"largely negotiated\" a week later is the sharpest trajectory change since the ceasefire. Iran's Foreign Ministry described the talks as both \"very far and very close\" — a formulation that maps genuine ambiguity rather than disguises it. Hormuz control remains the central sticking point: Washington wants the strait fully open; Tehran is seeking a supervision zone. US Central Command reported the naval blockade had stopped 89 commercial vessels since mid-April, though traffic last week doubled from the prior week.\n\nThe most structurally notable development was quiet. Mohammed bin Zayed and Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman participated in the same Oval Office call convened by the American president — alongside Qatar's emir Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani, Recep Tayyip Erdogan, Egypt's president, Jordan's king, Bahrain's king, and Pakistan's army chief. The two Gulf rivals have not been reported to have spoken bilaterally since what analysts have described as a diplomatic divorce; here they sat in the same US-convened framework. That does not repair the rupture. It maps its ceiling: bilateral hostility stops short of blocking joint participation in formats the Americans manage.\n\nThe economic track carried its own clarity. Sultan Al Jaber, the ADNOC chief executive, told an Atlantic Council audience that even if the conflict ended tomorrow, full Hormuz flows would not return before the first or second quarter of 2027, and that getting back to 80% of pre-conflict levels would take at least four months. He confirmed the West-East Pipeline — bypassing Hormuz to double export capacity through Fujairah — is 50% complete and being accelerated toward 2027 on the direct orders of Crown Prince Khaled bin Mohamed. When finished, the pipeline will handle roughly 3 million barrels per day. Hormuz normally carries 20 million. The infrastructure gap is structural and will remain so.\n\nAnwar Gargash, the presidential adviser, offered the clearest public framing yet of why the UAE left OPEC: the decision was three years in the making, rooted in the view that the world is approaching \"the autumn of the hydrocarbon age.\" The old OPEC quota of roughly 3.5 million barrels per day capped a country capable of producing 4.85 million — a 38% ceiling now removed. \"If you have the ability to produce and generate income and use that income in other investments, that's what you should do,\" Mr Gargash said.\n\nThose other investments continued. Mubadala committed $650 million — with a further $850 million from a multilateral consortium including the World Bank Group's IFC and IDB Group's BID Invest — to begin construction of a sustainable aviation fuel biorefinery in Bahia, Brazil, part of a broader $12.5 billion biofuels ambition there, with production expected from 2029. Core42, G42's sovereign cloud subsidiary, closed $550 million in structured trade finance with HSBC to accelerate AI infrastructure deployments across the US and Europe. Both are executed financings, not term sheets: capital committed, construction beginning.\n\nOne development remains unconfirmed but logged. Deutsche Welle, citing a Middle East Eye report based on two unnamed US officials, reported that Israel and the UAE agreed to establish a joint defense fund for weapons procurement during a covert visit by Israel's prime minister to Abu Dhabi — a visit the UAE has denied occurred. The US Ambassador to Israel separately confirmed that Israel loaned the UAE aerial defense weaponry. If the fund report is ever confirmed, it would represent the most formal institutionalization of a covert defense relationship that has been documented, piecemeal, since April.\n\nA peace deal, if it materializes, would reduce the immediate threat. It would not resolve the Hormuz infrastructure damage timeline, the bilateral rupture with Saudi Arabia, or the shape of the Iran relationship once the guns stop. The UAE has spent this week building the institutional record — in New York, London, Berlin, Luxembourg, Prague — that will matter when those questions come due.",
